<question>
A sequence of numbers $a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots$ is defined by $a_1 = 1$ and for all $n \geq 1$,
\[a_{n+1} = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
a_n + \frac{1}{n} & \text{if $n$ is a prime number}, \\
a_n - \frac{1}{n} & \text{if $n$ is a composite number}.
\end{array}
\right.\]

Find the smallest positive integer $k$ such that $a_k = a_1$.

Given that  $n$  is a positive integer, and  $f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cc}
x - \frac{1}{n}, & x \in \left[\frac{1}{n},1 \right]\\
0, & x \in \left[-\frac{1}{n},0\right] \cup (1,+\infty)\\
\end{array}
\right.$   $\left(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n+1}\right) \in (0,1]$ is a function that satisfy  $x_{k+1} \geq f(x_{k})$ . Find the minimum of  $\prod_{k=1}^{n}x_{k} + \frac{1}{\prod_{k=1}^{n}x_{k}}$ .
